] [17592186436220 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e7e78d0f-3ddc-5e29-ac7f-d4654fbb2dea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436221 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436221 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436221 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436221 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436221 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436221 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436221 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436221 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186436173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436221 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "efcaebab-808b-586a-b686-8d0602967092" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436222 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436222 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143566" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436222 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436222 #edb/ref 1071 "In a toy store, a total of $80$ green and red marbles are distributed between three boxes, $A,B,$ and $C.$ Box $A$ contains $16$ red marbles, while box $B$ contains $14$ red marbles. If $50%$ of all the marbles are red, what is the probability that a marble chosen at random is a red marble from box $C?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436222 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "115e7a48-2bd1-5ad1-83e6-8c6a3be144c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436222 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $R$ be the event that the selected marble is red, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the selected marble is from box $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the selected marble is from box $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the selected marble is from box $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nRecall that the law of total probability states that\n\n$$\nP(R)=P(R\\cap A)+P(R\\cap B)+P(R\\cap C).\n$$\n\nWe wish to find $P(R\\cap C):$\n\n- To calculate $P(R\\cap A),$ we take the number of red marbles in box $A$ $(16)$ and divide by the total number of marbles $(80).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(R\\cap A)=\\frac{16}{80}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(R\\cap B),$ we take the number of red marbles in box $B$ $(14)$ and divide by the total number of marbles $(80).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(R\\cap B)=\\frac{14}{80}.\n$$\n\n- Also, we're given that\n\n$$\nP(R)=50%=\\frac{40}{80}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(R\\cap C),$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R\\cap A)+P(R\\cap B)+P(R\\cap C) \\\\ \\frac{40}{80} & =\\frac{16}{80}+\\frac{14}{80}+P(R\\cap C) \\\\ P(R\\cap C) & =\\frac{40}{80}-\\frac{16}{80}-\\frac{14}{80} \\\\ P(R\\cap C) & =\\frac{10}{80} \\\\ P(R\\cap C) & =12.5%\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436223 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436223 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143995" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436223 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436223 #edb/ref 1071 "A company manufactures NY Yankees caps at three different plants. Plant $A$ produces $36%$ of the caps, of which $25%$ are white, Plant $B$ produces $34%$ of the caps, of which  $40%$ are white, and Plant $C$ produces the remaining $30%$ of all the caps. If the probability that a randomly selected cap from any of the three plants is white is $0.46,$ what is the probability that a randomly selected cap from plant $C$ is white?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436223 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "14105181-c45b-52bb-8273-83f863c9ff48" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436223 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $W$ be the event that a randomly selected cap is white, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the cap is from plant $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the cap is from plant $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the cap is from plant $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected cap from any of the three plants is white, we can use the law of total probability:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W) & =P(W\\cap A)+P(W\\cap B)+P(W\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(W|A)P(A)+P(W|B)P(B)+P(W|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since plant $A$ produces $36%$ of all the caps, plant $B$ produces $34%$ of all the caps, and plant $C$ produces $30%$ of all the caps, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.36,\\,P(B)=0.34,\\,P(C)=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $25%$ of the caps from plant $A$ and $40%$ of the caps from plant $B$ are white, we have\n\n$$\nP(W|A)=0.25,\\,P(W|B)=0.4.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected cap from any of the three plants is white is $0.46,$ we have \n\n$$\nP(W)=0.46.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(W|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W) & =P(W|A)P(A)+P(W|B)P(B)+P(W|C)P(C) \\\\ 0.46 & =(0.25)(0.36)+(0.4)(0.34)+P(W|C)(0.3) \\\\ 0.46 & =0.09+0.136+0.3P(W|C) \\\\ 0.3P(W|C) & =0.234 \\\\ P(W|C) & =0.78\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected cap from plant $C$ is white is $0.78.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436224 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436224 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143872" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436224 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436224 #edb/ref 1071 "A company manufactures electronic components. Of the component population, $45%$ are produced by machine $A,$  $25%$ by machine $B,$ and $30%$ by machine $C.$ If $5%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ and $3%$ of those produced by machine $B$ are defective, and the probability that a randomly selected component is defective is $0.042,$ what is the probability that a randomly selected component produced by machine $C$ is defective?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436224 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "199cacb7-9e7a-5d07-af35-fc181af78213" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436224 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly selected component is defective, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected component is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation. \n\n- Since machine $A$ produces $45%$ of the components, machine $B$ produces $25%$ of the components, and machine $C$ produces $30%$ of the components, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.45,\\,P(B)=0.25,\\,P(C)=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $5%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ and $3%$ of the components produced by machine $B$ are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.05,\\,P(D|B)=0.03\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected component is defective is $0.042,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.042.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(D|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\ 0.042 & =(0.05)(0.45)+(0.03)(0.25)+P(D|C)(0.3) \\\\ 0.042 & =0.0225+0.0075+0.3P(D|C) \\\\ 0.3P(D|C) & =0.012 \\\\ P(D|C) & =0.04\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected component produced by machine $C$ is defective is $0.04.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436225 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436225 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143748" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436225 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436225 #edb/ref 1071 "A company produces replacement batteries for a particular cell phone model at three locations. Location $A$ produces $30%$ of the batteries, of which $3%$ are defective. Location $B$ produces $38%$ of the batteries, of which $5%$ are defective. And location $C$  produces $32%$ of the batteries, of which also $5%$ are defective. What is the probability that a randomly selected battery is defective?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436225 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "296a9d4d-087e-5552-b718-aa436d5fbac8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436225 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $D$ be the event that the battery is defective, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the battery was produced at location $A,$\n\n- let $B$  be the event that the battery was produced at location $B,$\n\n- let $C$  be the event that the battery was produced at location $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected battery is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since  location $A$ produces $30%$ of the batteries,  location $B$ produces $38%$ of the batteries, and location $C$ produces $32%$ of the batteries, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.3,\\,P(B)=0.38,\\,P(C)=0.32.\n$$\n\n- Since $3%$ of the batteries produced at location $A$, $5%$ of the batteries produced at location $B$, and $5%$ of the batteries produced at  location $C,$ are defective, we have \n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.03,\\,P(D|B)=0.05,\\,P(D|C)=0.05.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected battery is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\  & =(0.03)(0.3)+(0.05)(0.38)+(0.05)(0.32) \\\\  & =0.009+0.019+0.016 \\\\  & =0.044.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436226 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436226 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143723" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436226 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436226 #edb/ref 1071 "Among all the people that visited Bill's ice cream shop last weekend, $25%$ were females under $18,$ $20%$ were females between $18$ and $30,$ and $15%$ were females over $30.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected person who visited Bill's shop last weekend is female?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436226 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3be4391a-3aa5-5bc8-9302-e0ef613d626c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436226 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $F$ be the event that a person selected was a female.\n\n- let ${A}_{1}$ be the event that the person was under $18$,\n\n- let ${A}_{2}$ be the event that the person was at least  $18$ but under $30$,\n\n- let ${A}_{3}$ be the event that the person was at least $30$ or older.\n\nThen the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected person was a female, we use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(F)=P(F\\cap {A}_{1})+P(F\\cap {A}_{2})+P(F\\cap {A}_{3})\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nP(F\\cap {A}_{1})=0.25,\\,P(F\\cap {A}_{2})=0.2\\,P(F\\cap {A}_{3})=0.15.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected person is a female is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F) & =P(F\\cap {A}_{1})+P(F\\cap {A}_{2})+P(F\\cap {A}_{3}) \\\\  & =0.25+0.2+0.15 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436227 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436227 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143626" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436227 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436227 #edb/ref 1071 "A company manufactures three types of cars. Of the car population, $40%$ are model ${C}_{1},$  $35%$ are model ${C}_{2},$ and the remaining cars are model ${C}_{3}.$ If $6%$ of the model ${C}_{1},$ $4%$ of the model  ${C}_{2},$ and $2%$ of the model ${C}_{3}$ cars break down during the first year, what is the probability that a randomly selected car will break down during the first year?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436227 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "507f5970-478a-564c-a05d-7f723ba11f01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436227 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $B$ be the event that a randomly selected car breaks down during the first year, and \n\n- let ${C}_{1}$ be the event that the car is of model ${C}_{1},$\n\n- let ${C}_{2}$ be the event that the car is of model ${C}_{2},$\n\n- let ${C}_{3}$ be the event that the car is of model ${C}_{3}.$\n\nThen the disjoint union ${C}_{1}⊔{C}_{2}⊔{C}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected car will break down during the first year, we can use the law of total probability: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B) & =P(B\\cap {C}_{1})+P(B\\cap {C}_{2})+P(B\\cap {C}_{3}) \\\\  & =P(B|{C}_{1})P({C}_{1})+P(B|{C}_{2})P({C}_{2})+P(B|{C}_{3})P({C}_{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $40%$ of the cars produced by the company are model ${C}_{1},$ and $50%$ are model ${C}_{2},$  the model ${C}_{3}$ cars make up the remaining\n\n$$\n100%-(40%+35%)=25%.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\nP({C}_{1})=0.4,\\,P({C}_{2})=0.35,\\,P({C}_{3})=0.25.\n$$\n\n- Since $6%$ of the cars of model ${C}_{1},$ $4%$ of those of model  ${C}_{2},$ and $2%$ of the cars of model ${C}_{3}$ break down during the first year, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|{C}_{1})=0.06,\\,P(B|{C}_{2})=0.04,\\,P(B|{C}_{3})=0.02.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected car breaks down during the first year is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B) & =P(B|{C}_{1})P({C}_{1})+P(B|{C}_{2})P({C}_{2})+P(B|{C}_{3})P({C}_{3}) \\\\  & =(0.06)(0.4)+(0.04)(0.35)+(0.02)(0.25) \\\\  & =0.024+0.014+0.005 \\\\  & =0.043.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436228 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436228 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143753" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436228 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436228 #edb/ref 1071 "In a gas station, $40%$ of the clients buy Regular gasoline, $45%$ buy Premium gasoline, and $15%$ buy Supreme gasoline. If $65%$ of the clients that purchase Regular gasoline paid with a credit card, $50%$ of the clients that buy Premium gasoline paid with a credit card, and $60%$ of the clients that buy Supreme gasoline paid with a credit card,  what is the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436228 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7c588063-b6d3-5525-ae01-ed797a4d9cc8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436228 #edb/ref 1074 "Let  $C$ be the event that the client paid with a credit card, and\n\n- let $R$ be the event that the client bought Regular gasoline,\n\n- let $Q$  be the event that the client bought Premium gasoline,\n\n- let $S$ be the event that the client bought Supreme gasoline.\n\nThen the disjoint union $R⊔Q⊔S$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C\\cap R)+P(C\\cap Q)+P(C\\cap S) \\\\  & =P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|Q)P(Q)+P(C|S)P(S)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $40%$ of the clients buy Regular gasoline, $45%$ of the clients buy Premium gasoline, and $15%$ of the clients buy Supreme gasoline, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.4,\\,P(Q)=0.45,\\,P(S)=0.15.\n$$\n\n- Since $65%$ of the clients that buy Regular gasoline, $50%$ of the clients that buy Premium gasoline, and $60%$ of the clients that buy Supreme gasoline,  paid with a credit card, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|R)=0.65,\\,P(C|Q)=0.5,\\,P(C|S)=0.6.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|Q)P(Q)+P(C|S)P(S) \\\\  & =(0.65)(0.4)+(0.5)(0.45)+(0.6)(0.15) \\\\  & =0.26+0.225+0.009 \\\\  & =0.575.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436229 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436229 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143560" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436229 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436229 #edb/ref 1071 "Among the people arrested in a particular city in 2010, $16%$ were males under $18,$ $30%$  were males between $18$ and $40,$ and $24%$ were males over $40.$ What is the probability that a person randomly selected from all those arrested in 2010 is male?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436229 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a8bdf577-59f8-5a92-b08a-72c7b5bf639b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436229 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $M$ be the event that a person selected was a male, and\n\n- let ${A}_{1}$ be the event that the person was under $18$,\n\n- let ${A}_{2}$ be the event that the person was between $18$ and $40$, and\n\n- let ${A}_{3}$ be the event that the person was over $40.$\n\nThen the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected person is male, we use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(M)=P(M\\cap {A}_{1})+P(M\\cap {A}_{2})+P(M\\cap {A}_{3})\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nP(M\\cap {A}_{1})=0.16,\\,P(M\\cap {A}_{2})=0.3\\,P(M\\cap {A}_{3})=0.24.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected person is male is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(M) & =P(M\\cap {A}_{1})+P(M\\cap {A}_{2})+P(M\\cap {A}_{3}) \\\\  & =0.16+0.3+0.24 \\\\  & =0.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436230 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436230 #edb/ref 1069 "q-144013" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436230 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436230 #edb/ref 1071 "Three similar bags, $A,\\,B$, and $C,$ contain balls. Bag $A$ contains $4$ red and $6$ green balls, and bag $B$ contains $6$ red and $4$ green balls. A bag is selected at random, and a ball is randomly selected from the bag. If the probability that the selected ball is green is $\\frac{3}{5},$ what proportion of balls from bag $C$ are green?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436230 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ae1e8adc-b949-5f9d-889f-68a0d9d9eba2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436230 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $G$ be the event of selecting a green ball at random from one of the three bags, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the ball is from bag $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the ball is from $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the ball is from bag $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability of selecting a green ball, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(G) & =P(G\\cap A)+P(G\\cap B)+P(G\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|B)P(B)+P(G|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since a bag is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(B)=P(C)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since there are $6$ green balls out of $10$ in bag $A,$ and $4$ green balls out of $10$ in bag $B,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(G|A)=\\frac{6}{10},\\,P(G|B)=\\frac{4}{10}.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability of choosing a green ball is $\\frac{3}{5},$ we have\n\n$$\nP(G)=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(G|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(G) & =P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|B)P(B)+P(G|C)P(C) \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & =(\\frac{6}{10})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{4}{10})(\\frac{1}{3})+P(G|C)(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{2}{15}+(\\frac{1}{3})P(G|C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(G|C) & =\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{2}{15} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(G|C) & =\\frac{4}{15} \\\\ P(G|C) & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{4}{5}$ of the balls in bag $C$ are green." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436231 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436231 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143747" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436231 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436231 #edb/ref 1071 "Three bags contain marbles. Bag $1$ contains $12$ red marbles and $8$ green marbles. Bag $2$ contains $14$ red marbles and $6$ green marbles. And bag $3$ contains $10$ red marbles and $10$ green marbles. A bag is selected at random, and a marble is randomly selected from the bag. What is the probability that the marble is red?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436231 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ca1787dc-fa3e-5ab1-99c5-cbfbd849d0c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436231 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $R$ be the event of selecting a red marble at random from one of the three bags, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the marble is from bag $1,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the marble is from bag $2,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the marble is from bag $3.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability of selecting a red marble at random from one of the three bags, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R\\cap A)+P(R\\cap B)+P(R\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|B)P(B)+P(R|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since a bag is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(B)=P(C)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since there are $12$ red marbles out of $20$ in bag $1,$ $14$ red marbles out of $20$ in bag $2,$ and $10$ red marbles out of $20$  in bag $3,$ we have \n\n$$\nP(R|A)=\\frac{12}{20},\\,P(R|B)=\\frac{14}{20},\\,P(R|C)=\\frac{10}{20}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability of selecting a red marble at random from one of the three bags is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|B)P(B)+P(R|C)P(C) \\\\  & =(\\frac{12}{20})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{14}{20})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{10}{20})(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{60}+\\frac{14}{60}+\\frac{10}{60} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{60} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436232 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436232 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143877" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436232 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436232 #edb/ref 1071 "A company manufactures three types of cars. Of the total car population, $40%$ are model ${M}_{1},$ $35%$ are model ${M}_{2},$ and $25%$ are model ${M}_{3}.$ If $6%$ of model ${M}_{1}$ and $4%$ of model ${M}_{2}$ cars break down during the first year, and the probability that a randomly selected car from any of the three models breaks down during the first year is $0.045,$ what percentage of model ${M}_{3}$ cars will break down during the first year?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436232 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cad3485a-4b9b-57e5-9ec4-43f98bf6be7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436232 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $B$ be the event that a randomly selected car breaks down during the first year, and \n\n- let ${M}_{1}$ be the event that the car is model ${M}_{1},$\n\n- let ${M}_{2}$ be the event that the car is model ${M}_{2},$\n\n- let ${M}_{3}$ be the event that the car is model ${M}_{3}.$\n\nThen the disjoint union ${M}_{1}⊔{M}_{2}⊔{M}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected car will break down during the first year, we can use the law of total probability: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B) & =P(B\\cap {M}_{1})+P(B\\cap {M}_{2})+P(B\\cap {M}_{3}) \\\\  & =P(B|{M}_{1})P({M}_{1})+P(B|{M}_{2})P({M}_{2})+P(B|{M}_{3})P({M}_{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $40%$ of cars are model ${M}_{1},$, $35%$ are model ${M}_{2}$, and $25%$ are model ${M}_{3},$ we have\n\n$$\nP({M}_{1})=0.4,\\,P({M}_{2})=0.35,\\,P({M}_{3})=0.25.\n$$\n\n- Since $6%$ of model ${M}_{1}$ and $4%$ of model  ${M}_{2}$ break down during the first year, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|{M}_{1})=0.06,\\,P(B|{M}_{2})=0.04.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected car breaks down during the first year is $0.045,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=0.045.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(B|{M}_{3}),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B) & =P(B|{M}_{1})P({M}_{1})+P(B|{M}_{2})P({M}_{2})+P(B|{M}_{3})P({M}_{3}) \\\\ 0.045 & =(0.06)(0.4)+(0.04)(0.35)+P(B|{M}_{3})(0.25) \\\\ 0.045 & =0.024+0.014+0.25P(B|{M}_{3}) \\\\ 0.25P(B|{M}_{3}) & =0.007 \\\\ P(B|{M}_{3}) & =0.028=2.8%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2.8%$ of model ${M}_{3}$ cars break down during the first year." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436233 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436233 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143749" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436233 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436233 #edb/ref 1071 "Three companies supply widgets to a car shop. Company $A$ supplies $45%$ of the widgets, of which $2%$ are defective. Company $B$ provides $40%$ of the widgets, of which  $3%$ are defective. And company $C$ supplies the remaining widgets, of which $4%$ are defective. What is the probability that a randomly chosen widget from these companies will turn out to be defective?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436233 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cb95f880-a6d7-5a6d-8e15-40d3ec490c19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436233 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $D$ be the event that the widget is defective, and  \n\n- let $A$ be that the event that the widget was produced by company $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be that the event  that the widget was produced by company $B$,\n\n- let $C$ be that the event that the widget was produced by company $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.  \n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly  selected widget  is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since Company $A$ supplies $45%$ and Company $B$ produces $40%$ of the widgets, then Company $B$ produces the remaining\n\n$$\n100%-(45%+40%)=15%.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.45,\\,P(B)=0.4,\\,P(C)=0.15.\n$$\n\n- Since $2%$ of the widgets produced by Company $A$, $3%$ of the widgets produced by Company $B$, and $4%$ of the widgets produced by Company $C,$ are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.02,\\,P(D|B)=0.03,\\,P(D|C)=0.04.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected widget turns out to be defective is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\  & =(0.02)(0.45)+(0.03)(0.4)+(0.04)(0.15) \\\\  & =0.009+0.012+0.006 \\\\  & =0.027.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436234 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436234 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143629" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436234 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436234 #edb/ref 1071 "William has a desk with three drawers, $A,$ $B,$ and $C.$ In drawer $A,$ he has $14$ markers, of which $3$ are red. In drawer $B,$ he has $18$ markers, of which $4$ are red. And, in drawer $C,$ he has $20$ markers, of which $6$ are red. If one marker is selected at random from all the markers combined, what is the probability that it is red?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436234 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "da692c40-031f-5c36-9d03-46c939749133" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436234 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $R$ be the event that a chosen marker is red, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the marker was from drawer $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the marker was from drawer $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the marker was from drawer $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected marker is red, we use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(R)=P(R\\cap A)+P(R\\cap B)+P(R\\cap C)\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that William has a total of \n\n$$\n14+18+20=52\n$$\n\nmarkers in all three drawers.\n\n- To calculate $P(R\\cap A),$ we take the number of markers in the first drawer that are red $(3)$ and divide by the total number of markers $(52).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(R\\cap A)=\\frac{3}{52}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(R\\cap B),$ we take the number of markers in the second drawer that are red $(4)$ and divide by the total number of markers $(52).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(R\\cap B)=\\frac{4}{52}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(R\\cap C),$ we take the number of markers in the third drawer that are red $(6)$ and divide by the total number of markers $(52).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(R\\cap C)=\\frac{6}{52}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected marker is red is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R\\cap A)+P(R\\cap B)+P(R\\cap C) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{52}+\\frac{4}{52}+\\frac{6}{52} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{52} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
